WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.263 --> 00:00:02.180
SPEAKER: Hi, everyone.

00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:05.120
Thanks for joining us today
to see a quick demo of Migrate

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.670
for Anthos.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:09.580
This demo shows how a
multi-tier application running

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:14.470
on VM or on prem can be migrated
and run on GKE containers.

00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:17.380
It also shows how to update an
image using the Create a Doctor

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:20.020
file and how to deploy
the updated image

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:23.240
as part of day two operations.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:25.850
So just a little bit
of an orientation.

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:29.000
The demo covers an application
server and a database server

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.190
running on VM or on prem.

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:33.410
We're going to migrate
them to a GKE cluster

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:35.557
where the relevant
services, like load

00:00:35.557 --> 00:00:37.140
balancer and the
database application,

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.170
were moved as well.

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:42.020
We'll extract the image,
apply it to the cluster,

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.840
and then we'll go through an
image update using Cloud Build

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.750
and apply that update using
rolling upgrade to the cluster.

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:54.750
This is the VMware
deployment we have on prem.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.050
As you can see, the application
server and the database server

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:01.560
are running two different
operating systems, one SUSE

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.200
Linux and the other CentOS.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.070
And for the purpose
of this demo,

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:10.740
we already migrated the Suite
CRM database as a StatefulSet

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:15.660
and will now migrate the SUSE
web and application server.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.630
First, let's look at the
application running on prem.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:21.510
This is an open source
CRM application.

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:24.420
It's using a LAMP stack.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:27.780
You can see here the
application running.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:30.600
Now let's switch over to
the Kubernetes cluster side.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.740
And here you can see suitecrm-db
container already running

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:38.010
as a stateful set.

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:41.250
We also have the load balancer
setup for the application.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:42.980
And it will serve it
once the application

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:46.280
is executed on the cluster.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:49.060
Let's go and install the
Migrate for Anthos software

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:50.350
on that cluster.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:55.220
For that, we will use the
migctl setup install command.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:57.610
The migctl is available
on Cloud Shell.

00:01:57.610 --> 00:02:00.160
And the setup install command
will connect to the cluster

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.570
and apply the different
solution components.

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:06.240
You can then run
migctl doctor to see

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.130
that installation is complete.

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:11.190
And in that case, it will
return a check on deployment.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:13.710
If there are any issues, it
will be shown here instead

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:16.590
of the check on deployment.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:18.240
Next, we will
configure the source

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:21.390
from virtual machines that
are going to be migrated.

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:23.640
And you configure
multiple sources.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:27.720
In this demo, we will define
the VMware on prem as a source.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.300
So we'll configure the
manager address for Migrate

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.120
for Compute Engine and the
cloud extension to which we

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.180
want to connect to so that we
can use migrate for Compute

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:38.160
Engine as a
transport and you can

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.311
see the source as configured.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.270
Next, we'll create
a migration plan.

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:48.840
For this, we'll identify the
VM we would like to migrate.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.430
This is the VM we
saw on the VM side.

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:53.550
And since it is a
stateless application,

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:56.490
we will specify an image
for the intent flag

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:58.350
and then create the
migration command

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:00.930
to ask only for
image extraction.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.750
The migration plan is
written into a YAML file that

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.280
can be reviewed and customized.

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:17.860
In the YAML file, you'll
see the definitions

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:20.860
for the different artifacts
that are going to be generated,

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:23.920
as well as default filters
for exclusions of directories

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:27.580
and files that are not going
to be included in the image as

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:30.910
well as the target image
names for deploying into GCR

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:33.360
once these are built.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:35.820
Next, we're going to
generate the artifacts.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.620
Basically, feeding
this migration plan

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:40.080
into the Migrate for
Anthos will execute

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:43.560
the migration and the breakdown
of these different artifacts.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:45.040
For this, we will
use the makectl

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:47.850
generate artifacts command.

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:50.640
First, the migration
plan is validated.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.860
And then it's applied
to the cluster.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:55.710
The migration is
applied as a CRD

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:59.010
so you can also use
the CRD as an API

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:02.310
to automate this migration.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:05.370
Then you'll use the
makectl migration status

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:08.130
to monitor the status and
progress of this migration

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:09.690
throughout its
different activities.

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:22.190
What you'll see now is that
since we're communicating

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:25.340
with the on prem
VMware system, the VMs

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:28.790
that are still running on prem
will be picked into a snapshot.

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:32.180
And from that we will
start extracting the image.

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:34.790
Here we can see that the
image extraction applied

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:37.760
into GCR with the image
name that we configured

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:39.410
into the migration plan.

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:42.020
So we skipped the entire
upload process to GCR.

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:52.330
In addition, in a
bucket in GCS, you

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:55.360
will find the set of created
artifacts as doctor file

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:57.130
and deployment
artifacts that are

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:58.850
usable for further
deployment and day

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:04.430
two operations when you'd like
to update this image later.

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:06.050
To download the
artifacts, you can

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:09.890
use the migctl migration
get artifacts command

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:11.450
with the migration name.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:14.000
And you can see the deployment
spec that was automatically

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:17.510
created or the image that
was automatically built.

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:19.370
And this deployment
spec can be applied

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:21.860
to this or any other
cluster where you would

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:24.870
like to run these workloads.

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:26.630
So let's go ahead and
apply the deployment

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:28.422
so that we can see the
application running.

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:46.280
Now that the
deployment is applied,

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:49.280
it is inserted into the
environment we precreated.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:52.160
Just as a reminder, we have
a load balancer set up.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:54.690
And we also have an
autoscaler configured.

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:56.600
So when this deployment
goes into place,

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:58.910
the autoscaler kicks
in automatically

00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:02.570
and sets up a minimum and
maximum pods and the monitoring

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:07.310
on the CPU metrics according
to those conditions.

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:10.090
So you can immediately see some
of the Kubernetes capabilities

00:06:10.090 --> 00:06:10.600
in action.

00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:18.020
Now let's connect to
the load balancer.

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:20.050
And you can see the
application running.

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:22.340
We'll just connect to this
internal load balancer

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:24.785
and you can see the
application now running in GKE.

00:06:45.490 --> 00:06:47.050
Now that the
application is running,

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:50.188
we're switching to talk
about day two operations.

00:06:50.188 --> 00:06:51.730
Let's say that we
have a package that

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:54.280
is a user mode
package in the image

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:56.180
and we would like to update it.

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:58.600
In this case, we will
put the user mode package

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:01.810
for update in the doctor file
that we downloaded as part

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:03.850
of the generate artifacts.

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:08.020
This is, for example,
an Open SSH package.

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:10.450
Then this doctor file can
be fed into Cloud Build

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:12.610
with a single G Cloud command.

00:07:12.610 --> 00:07:14.470
And we get a new
built image that

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:17.590
includes the updated package.

00:07:17.590 --> 00:07:20.050
We're using here an
update for SUSE Linux.

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:22.870
So we're using the
package manager in Zipper

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:24.040
to update the image.

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:29.830
We want you to see
the image built.

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:31.450
We've accelerated this process.

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:34.173
It usually takes 12 to
13 minutes to complete,

00:07:34.173 --> 00:07:36.340
but we sped this up a bit
for the sake of this demo.

00:07:54.130 --> 00:07:56.140
Now that the image
is updated and built,

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:59.140
if we go to the repository,
we can see the updated image

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:01.130
version.

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:03.130
And we can go and
perform a rolling update

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:04.840
on the actual deployment.

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:07.840
Remember that we have now a
deployment with three pods

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:09.610
running the web
front end, so we're

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:12.670
going to apply a live update.

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:14.980
In this live update, you
will see the new version

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:17.470
being provisioned alongside
the old version and then

00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:19.690
the old version
being decommissioned.

00:08:19.690 --> 00:08:22.450
During this process, the
application is staying up.

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:29.720
And you can see that here.

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:32.240
While this process is
going on, the application

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:33.530
is still accessible.

00:08:33.530 --> 00:08:37.148
And in a few seconds, the update
process will be completed.

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:51.830
And now we can see the updated
image version has been applied.

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:54.110
And we did not have to
touch any of these instances

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:54.890
individually.

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:01.660
That concludes our demo of
Migrate for Anthos today.

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:04.230
Thank you so much for
taking the time to watch it.

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:07.640
And we look forward to more
demo videos in the future.

